# The words a message of `cbor` names a kind of item with: each case of the
# face's `Value`, by the name `kind_of` answers, read as RFC 8949 names it.
# The face's accessors say with them what they expected and what they found.

# The kind `kind`, a name `kind_of` answers, as RFC 8949 names it and with
# its article: `"binary"` is `"a byte string"`. A name that is no case's is
# written as it came, in backquotes.
function described(kind: str) -> str
    return match kind
        "unsigned"   => "an unsigned integer"
        "negative"   => "a negative integer"
        "binary"     => "a byte string"
        "text"       => "a text string"
        "list"       => "an array"
        "pairs"      => "a map"
        "tagged"     => "a tag"
        "float"      => "a float"
        "boolean"    => "a boolean"
        "null_value" => "null"
        "undefined"  => "undefined"
        "simple"     => "a simple value"
        _            => f"`{kind}`"

## Tests

test "described: every case's name, and a name that is none"
    assert described("binary") == "a byte string"
    assert described("pairs") == "a map"
    assert described("null_value") == "null"
    assert described("unsigned") == "an unsigned integer"
    assert described("simple") == "a simple value"
    assert described("") == "``"
    assert described("map") == "`map`"
